  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best solution is to <b>Submit log data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, and set your log-processing application as a consumer. Ensure your application can scale automatically to manage demand increases.</b> Kinesis allows you to ingest, buffer, and analyze streaming data in real time. By subscribing your processing application and enabling autoscaling, you ensure low-latency analytics that adapts to spikes in log data, satisfying the requirements for always up-to-date and real-time reporting while remaining cost-effective.</p> <p>Here's why the other options are less suitable:</p> <ul><li><i>Send your log files to an Amazon S3 bucket, and utilize AWS CloudFormation to deploy an Auto Scaling group for a post-processing application that downloads and analyzes the S3 logs.</i> — This setup processes logs in batches, not in real time, and there may be significant delays as the application must continually poll and download files from S3.</li> <li><i>Publish your application logs to CloudWatch Logs, and configure your application to autoscale according to the workload.</i> — While CloudWatch Logs is excellent for centralized log storage and alerting, querying log data in real time (such as generating user leaderboards) is cumbersome and may not meet strict low-latency requirements for real-time analytics.</li> <li><i>Implement a multi-AZ Amazon RDS MySQL cluster for storing logs, sending your logs directly to MySQL, and conduct map-reduce queries to pull the user metrics.</i> — RDS MySQL databases are not designed for high-frequency, real-time analytics on streaming data and map-reduce operations are not natively supported, making this option inefficient and potentially expensive at scale.</li></ul><p><b>Tips:</b> For real-time analytics on rapidly changing data, use streaming services like Kinesis over batch or polling-based architectures. Always ensure your architecture is designed for scalability to deal with unpredictable workloads.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your log analytics tool currently requires over four hours to compile a list of the top 10 most active users on your website. Management now needs near real-time reports that are always current, and the solution must automatically scale to accommodate surges in web traffic. Which approach is the most cost-effective and meets all these requirements?</p>",
